SM(581,{"n":"2 Timothy 1.1-18","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/2 Timothy 1.1-18.mp3","d":2888.1,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.76,"So we'll go ahead and uh get started."],[24.24,"When we get started here in just a moment, we are going to be starting uh St."],[28.88,"Paul's second epistle to St."],[31.6,"Timothy. We're not gonna have a big long introduction this time, because uh we had that at the beginning of First Timothy."],[39.84,"And it all still applies."],[41.92,"That's uh pretty much everything we said about First Timothy also applies to 2 Timothy."],[47.04,"This is this does what it says on the tin."],[50.16,"It's the second letter."],[52.96,"St. Paul wrote to St."],[54.08,"Timothy. He's gonna be also, I mean, even gonna be addressing a lot of the same themes."],[59.52,"It's the same type of letter."],[61.0,"It's a letter from St."],[61.88,"Paul to an individual person."],[63.72,"It's in that mentorship relationship that St."],[66.92,"Paul has with St."],[67.88,"Timothy, preparing him uh for continued ministry."],[73.08,"And once Saint Paul is gone, because St."],[76.04,"Paul's death is approaching, preparing St."],[77.96,"Timothy to sort of step into some of the roles that St."],[81.4,"Paul has been playing as an apostle while he's been alive and some of the churches that he has founded."],[90.84,"That's probably all we need to say in terms of introduction, and then we can just sort of dive right into it."],[97.24,"Well, I think."],[98.04,"Unless there are any other needs like a second one subjects."],[99.72,"s. Well, it' not identical"],[106.08,"He doesn't say identical things, but it's still within the same r it's not about something different."],[112.22,"Yeah. In the sense that it's not about like uh oh, I forgot to mention this or that, or now something has happened and he's addressing a particular event."],[123.1,"There's no provoking incident."],[125.28,"No. No. This is this is, you know, hey, uh, you know, I called you when we talked a few weeks ago, now I'm calling, so we can talk some more."],[137.12,"That's that kind of thing."],[138.64,"Yeah. Okay. So unless there are any other questions, comments, hurled insults, uh, we'll go ahead and pick up in uh St."],[151.0,"Paul's second epistle to St."],[152.76,"Timothy chapter one, verse one."],[157.88,"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, by the will of God, according to the promise of life which is in Christ Jesus."],[165.96,"Okay, so this is one of his usual addresses."],[169.96,"Um he's already planting a couple of little themes that he's gonna continue on as we move on into the letter."],[177.88,"Um as is common, he evokes God the Father and Christ."],[184.36,"Um here we see the idea of the will of God and uh the promise of life."],[193.32,"It is in Christ Jesus."],[194.96,"These are two separate themes that we're going to see as we go forward."],[200.96,"Verse 2 to Timothy, a beloved son."],[204.16,"We saw that father-son language all through 1 Timothy."],[208.0,"Grace, mercy, and peace from God the Father in Christ Jesus our Lord, sort of his standard intro."],[217.12,"Verse 3 I thank God whom I serve with a pure conscience, as my forefathers did, as without ceasing, I remember you in my prayers night and day, greatly desiring to see you, being mindful of your tears, that I may be filled with joy."],[234.44,"When I call to remembrance the genuine faith that is in you, which dwelt first in your grandmother Lois and your mother Eunice, and I am persuaded is in you also."],[247.24,"So there's an interesting phrase there in verse three."],[253.64,"Serves God with a pure conscience, as his forefathers did."],[269.32,"For example, in 1 Corinthians, when he said to a bunch of pagan converts to Christianity, remember, our fathers passed through the sea and were baptized into Moses."],[284.32,"Then went on in the same chapter in chapter 10 to talk about the fact that they weren't Jews."],[291.41,"That they were not Jews according to the flesh."],[294.1,"So this is part of St."],[296.66,"Paul's understanding of what the church, the community of God, is, as Israel, is that everyone who has come into the church is grafted into the root, remember, which was the faithfulness of Abraham in Romans."],[312.8,"So if you are in Christ, you have Abraham as your father, he says in Galatians."],[320.8,"So when he's talking about his forefathers, he's talking about his forefathers in faithfulness. not genetic forefathers"],[331.46,"Because his genetic forefathers didn't always serve God with a pure conscience."],[336.74,"In fact, most of St."],[337.7,"Paul's life he did serve God with a good conscience."],[342.98,"This is further evidence that he's not starting a new religion."],[346.88,"He sees what he's doing as being in complete continuity with what was happening with Abraham and with Moses and everyone else who has been faithful down through down through the line."],[361.28,"And he mentions that because he's now applying that directly to St."],[369.04,"Timothy's life. Because Saint Timothy is already, we know from the from 1 Timothy, remember talked about let no one despise you because of your youth."],[380.84,"He's still a young man."],[382.76,"He's now a third generation Christian."],[389.48,"So he mentions here his grandmother and his mother, saying they were faithful people."],[397.16,"He says, I know that same faithfulness is in you."],[400.68,"So he's drawing the connection from, by the will of God, St."],[405.09,"Paul is an apostle."],[406.13,"He's continuing this story that started all the way back with Abraham."],[409.41,"This line of faithfulness."],[410.69,"There is forefathers"],[412.29,"And then he's pointing St."],[413.57,"Timothy to in his own line."],[416.71,"His mother and his grandmother who went before him."],[419.61,"And who were faithful, and saying, you've received this inheritance quite literally within your family."],[426.63,"Do we know where these people live?"],[428.63,"Where Timothy's from?"],[430.79,"Uh I don't."],[433.67,"I don't think we're told a city."],[438.15,"But I will I will double check on that."],[447.49,"But so this is there's this living tradition that's not just church tradition, but that goes back into the Old Testament."],[454.94,"All the way back to the beginning."],[457.54,"On the macro scale, micro scale, St."],[460.02,"Timothy has this in his own in his own family."],[468.98,"Verse 6. Therefore, I remind you to stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands."],[476.9,"For God has not given us a spirit of fear, but of power and of love and of a sound mind."],[484.34,"So uh laying on of hands."],[488.58,"Uh, this is, of course, as we said, we he used this phrase in First Timothy also."],[493.7,"This isn't referring to uh throwing hands."],[497.16,"This isn't referring to getting in a fight."],[500.6,"This is laying hands on him."],[502.12,"This is ordination."],[504.5,"And in particular, we can tell verse 7."],[510.42,"Remember, like I'm always saying, spirit should almost always be capitalized in the New Testament."],[516.52,"But it's talking about the spirit God has given us."],[518.76,"It's not talking about we've got spirit."],[522.36,"Let's hear it."],[523.38,"The pep rally."],[524.35,"This is talking about the Holy Spirit, who he received from the laying on of hands from Saint Paul."],[531.09,"So Saint Paul is the one who ordained him and set him apart, and from whom he received the Holy Spirit."],[537.41,"And so it's the Holy Spirit he's talking about."],[539.81,"And the Holy Spirit is not a spirit of fear."],[542.95,"But of power and of love and of a sound mind."],[547.05,"And those three things go together."],[550.35,"So power and love are not opposed to each other if we're really talking about the Holy Spirit."],[557.72,"And a sound mind is not opposed to love if it's really coming from the Holy Spirit."],[565.8,"All three of these things cooperate."],[571.08,"And he's saying, so you've received the Holy Spirit, you need to now make use of the Spirit."],[578.46,"Do not receive the Holy Spirit in vain."],[580.8,"As he says elsewhere, do not receive the the grace of God in vain."],[584.56,"Stir up is an interesting expression."],[587.28,"Yeah Well, it's stir up as in not letting something settle."],[592.7,"Yeah. But I mean, we wouldn't probably pick those words, but the Greek."],[598.38,"Yeah, well, it's that it's that idea."],[600.84,"As opposed to like sediment."],[602.6,"So like don't let it like uh become sediment."],[607.36,"But stirred up meaning it's active, it's moving."],[611.76,"What was there understanding like the concept of power?"],[613.92,"Like, could you explain that?"],[615.84,"Well in that concept."],[617.68,"Well, that's the thing."],[618.64,"That's why it's important that these three are together."],[620.98,"Because Caesar had power."],[623.63,"Not so much with the love."],[626.05,"And in a lot of cases, depending on your Caesar, not sound mind."],[630.47,"If you're talking about Caligula, Nero."],[633.55,"Um so uh we've got these three things that are often opposed even now today."],[641.49,"In in our common thought."],[643.06,"Power is looked at almost as an evil, or at least as a corrupting influence."],[650.44,"Power is the ability to make things happen."],[653.69,"Um, and authority."],[655.3,"Those are looked at negatively a lot of the time."],[657.46,"And they're usually opposed to, for example, love."],[660.5,"Love is seen as very passive."],[663.32,"In our contemporary world, love is sort of just acceptance."],[669.14,"General goodwill toward people."],[673.52,"It's not anything real or powerful."],[677.84,"It's opposed to power."],[680.38,"Active. And, you know, God's love obviously is active."],[688.19,"God's love for us causes him to do things."],[690.59,"It causes him to act."],[694.01,"And parental love is probably a good."],[697.85,"I mean, we we refer to God as father for a reason."],[700.83,"Parental love is probably a good analogy in that sometimes, you know, a father or a parent who loves their child has to act out of that love in ways that the child doesn't necessarily like."],[713.85,"Um that that the child may see as an abusive power."],[719.19,"But which is motivated by love."],[721.68,"So those things are coupled."],[722.88,"So it's it's power, but it's power that's used for the purpose of love."],[726.96,"It's used for the purpose of bettering others, of trying to bring them to maturity and and perfection and completion."],[735.33,"And then the third piece is a sound mind."],[740.47,"That this isn't, and again, we tend to put, you know, thoughts."],[747.4,"Reason, logic, facts over here, and then feelings like love over here."],[753.4,"But St As if these are two different things and they're opposed to each other."],[758.63,"Paul is saying, no, they're not."],[761.05,"So if you love someone, you want what's best for them, and it's what's actually best for them."],[768.91,"It's what's actually good."],[771.47,"And you understand what's actually good by having a sound mind and being able to see clearly what good is and what good who God is and what God wants."],[780.93,"And so then once you've seen that, you should be motivated by love to bring that about for people."],[786.07,"And in order to make it happen, that requires power and authority."],[792.4,"And so for St."],[793.2,"Paul, the Holy Spirit that's been given to St."],[795.12,"Timothy has given him all three."],[800.04,"But even though he's been given all three, that doesn't necessarily mean he's going to exercise all three."],[807.96,"Might sit at home and play Xbox."],[813.32,"Not literally. But."],[815.58,"So they're kind of referring to his position as being ordained."],[819.03,"And say you need to go out and use what God has given you."],[822.95,"He's given you these gifts in order."],[826.31,"He's given you his spirit in order that you can make things happen."],[831.05,"And so don't like let that settle like sediment."],[835.03,"And just waste it."],[837.27,"And let it go to rot."],[838.63,"But go out there and actualize it stir it up, be active, be alive, be involved."],[844.35,"So he's not talking about like state chunk."],[846.43,"No. Yeah, it's not powers."],[851.71,"Yeah. It's not superpowers or magical powers."],[856.19,"Like you could you could do miracles, go do them."],[858.51,"You know, yeah."],[859.71,"Um. Yeah. Verse 8."],[865.71,"Therefore, do not be ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner, but share with me in the sufferings for the gospel according to the power of God."],[878.83,"So St. Paul is continuing to sort of juxtapose those things."],[883.55,"So it's not, the Holy Spirit is not a spirit of fear"],[883.9,"And therefore, he's saying to St"],[889.32,"Timothy, don't be ashamed of preaching the gospel, and don't be ashamed of me sitting here in prison."],[898.14,"About to be executed."],[899.5,"But notice he says that his sufferings for the gospel are according to the power of God."],[907.58,"And Saint Paul has talked about this before how God's strength is made perfect in his weakness."],[913.87,"So he's saying, yes, it looks like St."],[916.35,"Paul is a prisoner, he's helpless, he's suffering, he's gonna die."],[922.49,"But St. Paul is saying God is doing something in and through this."],[926.77,"And because God is doing something in and through this, you shouldn't be ashamed of it, you shouldn't be embarrassed of it, you shouldn't be afraid about what's going to happen."],[934.85,"God is acting through it, even though it may not be obvious."],[938.37,"And this, of course, St."],[939.65,"Paul repeatedly points out that the cross is sort of the ultimate symbol of this."],[945.15,"That the cross is a man naked being tortured to death, being executed by the Roman state."],[952.12,"It seems like a symbol of weakness and powerlessness and humiliation."],[955.8,"But in actuality, it's the glory of God because of what God is doing in and through the cross."],[963.58,"And so St."],[964.3,"Paul is saying that's playing out in his own life."],[967.1,"St. Timothy needs to learn to see that, not only in St."],[969.74,"Paul.'s life, but in his own"],[972.02,"That even when it seems like you're weak and powerless, God can be acting powerfully through what is happening."],[982.18,"Verse 9. Who has saved us?"],[984.9,"This is God, of course, who has saved us and called us with a holy calling, not according to our works, but according to his own purpose and grace, which was given to us in Christ Jesus before time began."],[998.5,"Okay. So, oh, here come all the Calvinists."],[1004.11,"But again, context is our friend."],[1007.49,"So what is St."],[1008.45,"Paul talking about?"],[1010.23,"St. Paul is talking about God working in and through us."],[1013.27,"And so this is just a continuation of that theme"],[1018.46,"It's not that St."],[1019.82,"Timothy was doing a really good job."],[1022.14,"And so God comes and awards him."],[1025.1,"Good job, St."],[1026.22,"Timothy. Keep up the good work."],[1028.78,"So get your sales figures up next quarter."],[1033.34,"It's that God has his own purpose."],[1038.4,"This is all the way back to that first verse."],[1040.26,"Where I said the themes would come back, but he said he was an apostle according to the will of God."],[1045.08,"God has a purpose."],[1047.0,"That he has something he wants, is going to do, something he is going to do in the world, and which he's calling Saint Timothy to be the means through which he does it."],[1055.64,"Timothy wants And so it's not about what St."],[1062.36,"It's not about Saint Timothy trying to do something or achieve something or setting goals, making a vision statement for his church."],[1070.1,"It's not about what St."],[1071.3,"Timothy may want to do or accomplish or brag about someday at the end of his career, you know, when they give the speeches."],[1078.1,"It's about what God is doing in the world and what he wants to do through Saint Timothy, and Saint Timothy getting on board and doing it."],[1087.64,"Because the grace, grace, remember, is the divine energies."],[1090.44,"It's God acting."],[1092.36,"That's why he says by his own purpose and grace."],[1095.64,"God has the purpose, and it's God who's doing it through you."],[1098.6,"you. It's not, it's not about"],[1102.84,"And that that was given to us in Christ Jesus, before before the world began."],[1108.05,"Meaning it's really not about you."],[1111.03,"This is God's purpose from the very beginning."],[1114.81,"And he's saying that he, St."],[1116.57,"Paul, you, Saint Timothy."],[1119.29,"We've been given this gift by God."],[1122.11,"Of being a part of this history, this family."],[1126.77,"This genealogy of faithful people, of being part of what God has been doing from the very beginning in the world."],[1132.93,"And he's asserted very strongly that Christ was around before time began."],[1139.46,"Yeah. Again, sorry, Barter, but I'll keep picking on him"],[1143.18,"Um, and then verse 10, but has now been revealed by the appearing of our Savior Jesus Christ, who has abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel."],[1163.2,"So he says, this has been true."],[1165.7,"This has always been God's purpose."],[1167.3,"It's always been his plan."],[1168.42,"It's always been what he's doing."],[1170.18,"And now in our own day, that purpose has been revealed to us."],[1175.96,"It was a little unclear before."],[1177.98,"We weren't sure."],[1179.02,"We had images, we had glimpses, but now it's been fully revealed."],[1182.86,"We know what that is because we've seen Christ."],[1183.1,"He describes what Christ has done"],[1188.1,"He's abolished death and brought life and immortality to light through the gospel."],[1193.06,"Notice it doesn't say anything about anybody being punished for sins."],[1196.5,"It doesn't say anything about sin at all."],[1200.07,"So for these purposes, St."],[1201.59,"Paul's focusing on that element."],[1203.29,"Christ's defeat of death, his destruction of death, his bringing of life and immortality through the gospel."],[1208.73,"And we've talked before the word gospel is uh an ancient Greek word that was used for the report of a victory."],[1218.99,"So that's what he's saying."],[1220.11,"It's it's the appeal to that victory idea"],[1223.65,"Through the victory of Christ, he has abolished. death and given life and immortality"],[1231.69,"Verse 11, to which, the gospel, to which I was appointed a preacher, an apostle, and a teacher of the Gentiles."],[1239.69,"So St. Paul says, the part I was given, by God in this whole plan, and purpose, was to go and preach and proclaim and teach uh this gospel to the nations."],[1254.9,"Who Christ is and what he had done to all the nations."],[1259.86,"Verse 12 for this reason I also suffer these things."],[1263.54,"Nevertheless, I am not ashamed, for I know whom I have believed, and am persuaded that he is. able to keep what I have committed to him until that day"],[1275.66,"So he's not ashamed of where he is right now because he knows what's going on."],[1281.68,"He understands what God is doing."],[1284.16,"And more importantly, he knows Christ."],[1288.74,"That's that. I know whom I have, whom I have believed."],[1295.22,"The one to whom he's been faithful."],[1297.8,"The one who he's faithfully following."],[1299.64,"He knows Christ."],[1300.92,"And so he trusts him."],[1303.24,"He talks about what he's committed to him for that day."],[1305.72,"We saw St."],[1306.44,"Paul in 1 Timothy use language that was very reminiscent, the the idea of building up treasures in heaven."],[1311.8,"Remember when he was talking to the wealthy members. of the uh the of the church that St"],[1317.36,"Timothy was going to address."],[1318.89,"That idea of building up treasures in heaven rather than on earth."],[1322.01,"That's the idea that St."],[1323.21,"Paul's talking about here."],[1324.25,"What he's committed to Christ for that day, the day of the Lord, the day of judgment."],[1330.03,"So St. Paul's saying I'm not I'm not working for a reward here, but I know Christ, the one who I've been faithfully following, and so I know that he's going to be faithful to me when that day comes right in there software."],[1347.39,"Yeah we proclaim another message like the world and this is called that's a weakness by darkness and light of light he's preaching about life and why is he suffering telling people you know that death has been defeated and immortality is here but yeah he's suffering it makes that right yeah push it back."],[1371.51,"'Cause that's cause that's how Christ defeated it."],[1374.47,"Yeah. Christ defeated death by dying, by suffering and dying."],[1379.21,"Yeah. But he's not like people don't know."],[1381.61,"Yeah. Yeah. No no."],[1384.17,"Yeah, no, that's not a that's that's not a pit."],[1387.21,"There used to be uh, you know, the old four spiritual law tracks."],[1390.33,"I don't know if anybody's ever seen those anymore."],[1392.09,"I'm an old man now."],[1393.05,"But back in the day, the old four spiritual laws tracks from Campus Crusade."],[1397.85,"The first one this this was there, there were four principles you're supposed to present this track to people, and this was their summary of the gospel."],[1402.41,"The first one was God loves you and has a wonderful plan for your life"],[1410.21,"And back in the day, people used to, you know, they'd post a picture of St."],[1413.73,"Ignatius of Antioch being eaten by lions, and then they put God loves you and has a wonderful plan for your life, or St."],[1420.05,"John the Forerunner being beheaded."],[1422.19,"You know, like yeah, so yeah, people want to hear more like that."],[1427.07,"Like, oh really?"],[1427.95,"Does that include a lot of money?"],[1429.47,"Yeah. Uh but yeah, they they don't want to hear, you know, pick up your cross and and follow Christ."],[1440.65,"To be crucified."],[1442.17,"Very much the idea is like you're gonna have to follow this horrible suffering and to find eternal life that is not present."],[1446.57,"That's what pick up your cross and follow me means"],[1449.01,"Yeah. Yeah, yeah."],[1454.21,"You're gonna suffer a lot."],[1455.69,"You're gonna, yeah."],[1456.75,"You're gonna you're gonna be you're gonna live the kind of life that Christ lived."],[1461.39,"Which, you know, a lot of rejection, a lot of mockery, a lot of ending in beatings and execution."],[1472.43,"S I intend them water, but it was testifying."],[1477.99,"Because they were showing as for someone to be willing to die for the gospel, they had to believe what St."],[1484.71,"Paul is saying here that he believes."],[1488.71,"That where the time comes and you stand before the throne of Christ, that he's going to be faithful, to you."],[1495.09,"And so that's the ultimate testimony to that is the fact that you're willing to go to your death."],[1502.17,"Well, this is a paradox, but it's central to the whole Christian idea."],[1508.67,"Um, but the fact that it's a paradox and is irrational, um I think is one of the most appealing features of that."],[1518.59,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[1520.91,"Because it transcends our our reason."],[1523.47,"And that's why that's why uh Nietzsche is the greatest critic of Christianity."],[1528.91,"Because he's the only critic of Christianity who's ever really understood Christianity."],[1534.31,"Like he looked at Christianity and he saw, he said, there's this inversion here."],[1539.85,"Nietzsche was a classical philologist."],[1542.17,"That's actually what he was."],[1543.77,"And so he looked at the Greeks and the Romans of the classics."],[1547.29,"He's like, okay, see, I get the power."],[1549.61,"I get that."],[1550.63,"People are powerful, they're strong, they're beautiful, they're right, they're wealthy, all these good things."],[1556.87,"And then he looked at Christianity, he said, Christianity comes along and inverts all of that."],[1561.99,"And says that those people are evil, and that's wicked, and that these peasants, these suffering people, and these sick people, that these are good, and that this executive person is God."],[1573.61,"He says that's this inversion."],[1575.61,"And he hates that."],[1577.13,"But he at least got it."],[1578.83,"He at least saw it clearly."],[1581.73,"And uh so at least you could say he hated Christianity for what Christianity actually is."],[1586.87,"He saw that there was this change from the way the world looks at things."],[1592.39,"Um yeah. Is that it's kind of like the to commit yourself so much to this transcendent idea of perfectness and goodness that God is, it's so beyond us."],[1606.63,"It's sort of like uh you kind of have to die in this entire world, everything you think is good in this world of like material comforts and all that, it's like you're sort of willfully sacrificing yourself the same way Christ did to that higher good kind of thing."],[1627.71,"Yeah, you you give up good things to get better things."],[1634.11,"And this is this is why the West doesn't really get asceticism."],[1640.11,"There's a non-controversial statement."],[1642.91,"Um in the West, it's assumed that anything you give up for asceticism is bad."],[1651.73,"So you read the Augustinian tradition in the West, the reason celibacy is good, is because no matter what, there's always. something about sex that's kind of bad"],[1666.59,"No matter what you do, there's always something about it that's kind of bad."],[1670.45,"And you give up money, and possessions because no matter what you do with it, it's always kind of bad."],[1679.73,"Because there's no category for actual asceticism."],[1684.7,"You're supposed to stop sinning, period."],[1687.5,"It's not asceticism to give up sin."],[1690.38,"You're not supposed to sin at all."],[1693.03,"Uh that that that you just avoid that."],[1695.77,"Asceticism is giving up something good in order to pursue something better."],[1701.53,"A good thing of this world, a material good."],[1705.31,"A lesser good in order to pursue that which is truly good, that which is eternal."],[1711.92,"Um so that's that's part of the problem."],[1717.68,"Uh then when when you get to ethics and morality and how that's understood, it's why you get this very law based ethic in the West."],[1729.8,"Because again, it's everything is either bad or good."],[1733.94,"There can only be one good thing, and everything else, this is comes out of Platonism."],[1738.92,"That distinction implies opposition."],[1740.92,"So if we say that this thing is good, then anything else has to be at least a little bad."],[1745.48,"The good has to be one, because Plato told us so"],[1746.16,"There can't be two equally good things."],[1751.42,"Um, it's funny something that I've often noticed with Protestantism, and that is that they have a kind of negative asceticism."],[1760.22,"It's about what you don't do."],[1763.07,"You don't drink placards, dance, whatever it is in that church."],[1767.47,"It's all stuff that you don't do, not stuff that you actively go out and do."],[1772.92,"And so in Protestantism, for example, it's hard to find any place for sell the idea of celibacy."],[1779.54,"Because they say, Well, well, sex is either good or bad."],[1783.16,"And they'll say, W,ell it's bad outside of marriage, so you don't do it at all, and it's good in marriage."],[1788.04,"And then since it's good, it's wrong to not do it in marriage."],[1792.18,"Because there's only these two categories."],[1796.79,"And uh, so even somebody who's and and people experience this a lot on a very practical level in a lot of Protestant churches."],[1804.47,"If you're an adult who does isn't married, you get looked at like something's wrong with you."],[1809.83,"You know, like what are you, you know, weirdo?"],[1813.11,"What's going on?"],[1814.63,"Um, you know, because if marriage is good, then everything else is somehow not quite as good."],[1823.07,"Um so asceticism gets swept aside completely, like."],[1828.53,"Like in fact, if you practice any type of asceticism, you're saying that what you're abstaining from is bad somehow from that perspective."],[1837.83,"Because it's about it's about good and bad."],[1840.31,"But the the the um the problem of morality is not people go and do evil things."],[1848.25,"You go back to Aristotle, he says, everyone always does what is good."],[1854.17,"That's at the beginning of the Nicomachean ethics."],[1856.43,"That's where he starts."],[1857.39,"Everyone always does what is good."],[1859.87,"They disagree about what's good."],[1862.52,"But they're doing what they think is good."],[1865.06,"And so the problem is that we we look at the good things of this world, these temporal and temporary goods, and we treat them as if they're the ultimate good to be pursued at the exclusion of all else."],[1882.64,"And God is the only ultimate good."],[1887.36,"And the scriptures are clear, even though these are unpopular passages, that we have to pursue God as the good at the exclusion of all else."],[1897.28,"Including when Christ says, if you love your father or mother more than me, you're not worthy of me."],[1903.04,"If you love your husband or wife more than me."],[1904.92,"If you love your children more than me, you're not worthy of me."],[1909.28,"So we pursue God to the exclusion of all else."],[1912.74,"But there are plenty of people in our world who pursue money to the exclusion of all else."],[1916.98,"They love it more than their children."],[1918.1,"They love it more than their spouse"],[1920.46,"They love it more than their parents."],[1922.0,"Or success or fame, or you know, uh countless other things."],[1928.41,"Pleasure. Enjoyment. They make that the ultimate good, and they're willing to throw everything else away to get it."],[1937.79,"Because that's part of how humans are built."],[1941.04,"But when you're wrong about what the ultimate good is, you end up doing evil."],[1947.6,"Because you're doing evil to all of those other people."],[1951.21,"You're doing evil to your family because you're busy trying to get rich."],[1956.49,"Or trying to get pleasure or trying to get whatever."],[1959.47,"And you're doing evil to all of those other people in pursuing what you've decided is the ultimate good."],[1965.15,"Whereas when we pursue God as the ultimate good, because of who God is, the spirit he places inside of us of a sound mind and of love and of power, we don't do evil to any of those people."],[1978.35,"It works in the exact opposite way."],[1981.23,"When we're pursuing God as the ultimate good to the exclusion of all else, we end up doing good and showing kindness and love to all of those other people."],[1991.63,"Even when we have to choose to follow God rather than what they want."],[1996.91,"We're still doing that with love and with kindness."],[2000.52,"Um, so yeah, that's the there's a there's a a uh major and important uh distinction and understanding there."],[2011.64,"Um the frankly, Western theology, I'm making non controversial statements tonight."],[2019.32,"Western theology has goes through what's called the Platonist period, following after St."],[2024.28,"Augustine. So think of like Bethius, and then culminating in like Anselm."],[2029.4,"And I don't think it ever recovered."],[2035.0,"So that's the Aristotle period."],[2040.12,"Yeah, yeah, which is theoretically better."],[2044.2,"I I like Aristotle better, but functionally, in terms of the theology you get that Platonist core was still the bedrock."],[2054.42,"Of like the understanding of the Trinity of all of these things."],[2059.54,"And so the Aristotelian tradition comes in and sort and nuances everything and explicates everything in new ways, but doesn't question or topple any of that stuff that went before."],[2073.3,"And so there are some core presuppositions there that took Western theology in the wrong direction, I would argue."],[2081.7,"Is that kind like of the small mistake thing?"],[2084.42,"All good and then anything distinct from that has to be bad."],[2089.28,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[2092.0,"Because unityity. simplic, is a perfection, according to Plato"],[2101.08,"And if God is all perfect, if good is perfect good, it has to possess that also."],[2107.56,"And so you can't have multiplicity, you can't have you can't have variation."],[2114.6,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[2119.08,"And so that that messes up everything."],[2121.08,"But say Saint Maximus the Confessor had a big dispute about this with Pyrrhus."],[2127.56,"And it was about Saint Maximus affirming that we would have free will in the eschaton, in the in the the life of world to come."],[2141.25,"Pyrrhus was arguing that we couldn't have free will."],[2144.85,"Because we'd only do good, and the good has to be one."],[2147.73,"It was that idea."],[2149.33,"So we would never make any choices."],[2152.37,"We would never, there would be no variation for everyone."],[2155.65,"Everyone and everything would be exactly the same because it would be good and it would be perfect, and that's simple."],[2160.93,"That sounds awful."],[2165.81,"Well that's the idea a lot of people have to have."],[2167.97,"Yeah, yeah. The angels that were good by he heavaveny."],[2173.89,"Why would there be more than one?"],[2176.53,"Yeah. The angels are good and an angel can't do angels"],[2182.97,"Well, other than the ones who are fallen."],[2184.65,"Yeah. Yeah. But there could it there could be there could be multiple, yeah."],[2191.37,"They're they're freely doing it, and there could be multiple goods."],[2194.89,"Oh. You could have variation that's still good."],[2201.07,"And one of the places we see this most obviously is the saints."],[2207.37,"When you look at icons of the saints, they all look kind of similar in style and stuff."],[2212.01,"Why is that?"],[2212.81,"Well, they're all conformed to the likeness of Christ."],[2215.13,"So they all look a little bit like Christ."],[2217.69,"But they also all are individual persons."],[2222.43,"They don't just become clones."],[2225.05,"Of each other or of"],[2225.61,"They're all different"],[2228.93,"And they had different lives and different gifts and did different things and had different experiences."],[2234.55,"So they show us thousands of ways to be just like Christ."],[2240.63,"And so that's where you get that freedom and variation."],[2244.31,"You just have to toss Plato in the dustbin, which I don't mind."],[2251.11,"Yeah. It's all in heaven you say potentially still."],[2255.59,"For us to be different."],[2257.11,"Okay, different. And that's not bad."],[2259.27,"That doesn't mean we're bad because we're different."],[2260.87,"Yeah, that's what I'm saying"],[2265.59,"Yeah. Yeah. With the ability, with the ability to do evil is purify from us to require us."],[2269.59,"Well, doing I won't say the ability, but uh options to do it"],[2276.67,"Well, that's it."],[2277.23,"See, so the the question is then how you see doing evil."],[2283.69,"So so doing evil, if you see doing evil as a failure to do good."],[2289.69,"A good mission."],[2291.22,"You're trying to do good."],[2292.5,"Everybody's trying to do good."],[2294.02,"Yeah. But we get confused and we don't see and we don't know what the good is, and so we fail."],[2300.04,"Well, obviously, if you're in heaven and you' putreting you're not going to fail at what you're trying to do."],[2304.04,"Oh, yeah So coming coming at it from coming at it from that perspective."],[2308.28,"But also St."],[2308.92,"Maximus again talks about how that version of the will, the way we talk about the will in um modern Western culture."],[2322.4,"When we talk about will and free will, we're talking about making choices."],[2325.7,"I can have Coke or Pepsi."],[2329.68,"Or Diet Coke or Coke Zero."],[2331.62,"I can I can uh we usually want two."],[2334.34,"That's an American culture thing, I think."],[2336.42,"We want like two of everything."],[2337.86,"We want like Coke and Pepsi, Xbox, PlayStation."],[2341.36,"Like Ford Chevy."],[2343.01,"Like you gotta have to have two so you have a choice."],[2346.69,"That's how we think of the will."],[2348.85,"Okay. St. Maximus says that's a second movement of the will that emerges after the fall into sin."],[2355.41,"It's a bug, not a feature"],[2363.93,"Having to sit and deliberate and trying to figure out what the right thing to do is."],[2369.99,"That's often a lesson."],[2371.43,"So he says Christ didn't have that."],[2377.11,"God doesn't have that."],[2378.31,"God doesn't have to make choices."],[2380.55,"He he knows."],[2381.99,"Again, sorry, Calvinist."],[2383.67,"He knows. He doesn't have to make choices."],[2388.39,"He acts eternally in accordance with his nature."],[2392.37,"And so for Saint Maximus, the reason we won't fall into sin. in the life of the world to come is that we'll have perfect free will"],[2404.49,"We'll finally be free to be and become who God created us to be."],[2409.61,"We'll be free from sin."],[2411.94,"Diverting that and breaking that down."],[2416.42,"Yeah, yeah. So the way the way will in the ancient world is a function of nature."],[2424.2,"So like if you if if you read Aristotle again, Aristotle talks about, he uses the word thelema uh uh in Greek."],[2432.52,"It's the same word for will."],[2434.2,"Talks about like trees having a will, rocks having a will."],[2439.98,"And you're like, what is he?"],[2442.1,"That's part of how movement works, but it it's it's also that it's related to their nature."],[2446.58,"So anything that has a nature, in a sense, has a will."],[2450.34,"And the will is sort of the internal force that moves it in the direction of that nature."],[2456.32,"So if you have an acorn, that acorn has the same nature as an oak tree."],[2462.18,"They're not different things."],[2463.7,"Like they look very different."],[2465.23,"But they have the same nature."],[2467.09,"So you go and you plant that acorn, and it's the will that leads the acorn then to is sort of the internal living force, that sort of drives it to grow, to move toward the sun."],[2479.49,"And all the process and it for gr to grow to fullness, and maturity as a as a full oak tree."],[2486.92,"So if that's how we understand the will, then in that analogy, sin is like rot or moss or parasites."],[2496.98,"That get into that tree somewhere in that process that would try to prevent the tree from becoming the full grown, mature, healthy oak tree."],[2510.1,"So if you take away those things, if you take away the sin, now that will is free to become."],[2515.7,"And so it's the same kind of idea with us, with our human nature."],[2520.56,"There's someone and something God created us to be and to become."],[2525.58,"And sin comes in sort. of parasitically, like rot, to try to stop us from getting there"],[2533.2,"And so once we're purified from that and healed from that, we're free."],[2538.0,"We have free will to grow into and become that person."],[2543.84,"On that context, real quick, like what would you say become the fruit of the knowledge of, you know, like just to tie that all together, that concept of the city of the world."],[2554.48,"Yeah, well, that was that the the tree of the knowledge of good and evil was a tree that we're told repeatedly, if you read Second Temple Jewish literature, first Enix being an example, is still in paradise and is not an evil thing."],[2569.28,"It's knowledge that Adam and Eve weren't ready for."],[2573.29,"Because this is another, this is another dividing line in a lot of Western Christianity from Easter Christianity is that traditionally Easter Christianity has seen Adam and Eve as innocent, as like babies."],[2586.59,"As like newborn children."],[2589.01,"Who are at the beginning of that journey to become what God created them to be."],[2594.21,"Whereas in the West, they tend to talk about Adam and Eve as being perfect."],[2599.33,"And then that creates this whole thing of like, well, how could perfect beings sin?"],[2605.23,"And you get into all that."],[2606.67,"And there's all kinds of things then going on there in Western theology."],[2609.87,"But if you understand that they're children, and then this is knowledge, there would have come a time in their maturity."],[2615.89,"Because God says to the angelic beings, they that man is now like us, knowing good and evil."],[2624.93,"So knowing good and evil didn't cause God to become evil."],[2629.81,"It didn't cause the elect angels to become evil."],[2634.14,"They're able to know good and evil."],[2635.68,"And so there would have come a time when humanity was mature enough to receive that knowledge."],[2642.93,"And so what the devil comes and says is if you if you eat it, you'll become like God."],[2650.45,"Why don't you do the mature grown up thing now?"],[2653.03,"Why don't you sound the temptation to children?"],[2656.55,"On your own effort."],[2660.09,"And so that's that's what's going on there is that they were trying to circumvent that process of growing and becoming."],[2670.14,"And just jump to, I want to be like God now."],[2675.55,"Okay. Verse 13."],[2680.75,"Hold fast the pattern of sound words which you have heard from me in faith and love, which are in Christ Jesus."],[2689.87,"That good thing which was committed to you, keep by the Holy Spirit who dwells in us."],[2696.35,"Okay. Um so he's he's saying, seriously, pattern of sound words."],[2703.61,"That's a a way of talking about."],[2706.35,"A way of describing the gospel of who Christ is, of who you are."],[2712.05,"That's what that pattern of sound words is."],[2714.85,"Um it's not like, okay, here's the exact words of the gospel, to recite."],[2720.84,"But it's a pattern."],[2722.66,"A way of seeing things and talking about things and describing things, and he's saying, You heard it from me, as you traveled with me, hold on to it."],[2731.0,"Faithfulness and love."],[2733.64,"And that's been committed to you."],[2735.32,"He just talked about how the gospel was committed to him, committed to Saint Paul, and he spent his life going and proclaiming it."],[2742.12,"He's saying, That's now been committed through me to you, Saint Timothy and you need to hold on to it."],[2752.72,"Verse 15. This you know that all those in Asia have turned away from me, among whom are Phagellus and Hermogenes."],[2761.12,"He's naming names."],[2773.28,"For he often refreshed me, and he was not ashamed of my chain."],[2777.92,"But when he arrived in Rome, he sought me out very zealously and found me."],[2782.56,"The Lord grant to him that he may find mercy from the Lord in that day."],[2786.68,"And you know very well how many ways he ministered to me at Ephesus."],[2791.96,"So he's saying, I had I had a lot of people at those churches in Asia."],[2797.58,"Which is Asia Minor, which is what's now Turkey, is what he's talking about."],[2801.58,"All those churches we saw him establishing in the book of Acts."],[2804.06,"He's like, I thought I had a lot of friends there."],[2806.22,"Uh found out, uh no."],[2809.11,"He names a couple of them who, you know, were acting like they were real supporters and have flaked out."],[2817.11,"But then he mentions Onesophoros and his family, who have done the opposite."],[2822.65,"They've he's been faithful."],[2823.93,"Not only has he been faithful in Asia, but he actually traveled to Rome and visited St."],[2828.49,"Paul when he was in prison."],[2830.83,"And then he's asking God to show mercy on him at the judgment the way he showed mercy on Saint Paul"],[2836.15,"Another connection to Christ's teaching."],[2839.45,"Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy."],[2843.15,"And the parable of the sheep and the goats."],[2845.33,"You visited me when I was in prison."],[2847.74,"These same kind of ideas."],[2848.94,"I always point these out because there are folks out there, uh, including all Muslims, who want to try and say St."],[2855.74,"Paul's teaching something totally different than Jesus, but actually there's all these overlaps all the time."],[2861.34,"This is another one of them."],[2865.66,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2873.02,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2874.3,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaithcom."]],"o":[{"t":21.76,"lv":2,"h":"Intro to 2 Timothy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>St Paul's second epistle to St Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No long intro - given at start of <b>1 Timothy</b>, \"all still applies\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Does what it says on the tin\" - the <b>second letter</b> Paul wrote to Timothy</li><li class=\"d1\">same themes, same type of letter: Paul to an <b>individual</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same <b>mentorship</b> relationship, preparing Timothy for continued ministry</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's <b>death approaching</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy to step into roles Paul played as apostle and in <b>churches he founded</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not identical - but not about something different either</li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>provoking incident</b>, no \"I forgot to mention\"; like \"I called you a few weeks ago, now I'm calling so we can talk some more\"</li></ul>"},{"t":157.88,"lv":2,"h":"2 Timothy 1:1-2 - Address","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:1</b> \"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ, by the will of God, according to the promise of life which is in Christ Jesus\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Usual address - already planting <b>themes</b> to continue</li><li class=\"d1\">evokes <b>God the Father and Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>will of God</b> and <b>promise of life</b> in Christ - two separate themes ahead</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:2</b> to Timothy, <b>\"beloved son\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">father-son language all through <b>1 Timothy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"grace, mercy, and peace\" - standard intro</li></ul>"},{"t":217.12,"lv":2,"h":"1:3-5 - Pure conscience, forefathers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:3-5</b>: thanks God \"whom I serve with a pure conscience, as my forefathers did\"; prays night and day; remembers Timothy's <b>tears</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>genuine faith</b> dwelt first in grandmother <b>Lois</b>, mother <b>Eunice</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Interesting phrase: <b>forefathers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cf. <b>1 Corinthians 10</b> - to pagan converts: \"our fathers passed through the sea and were baptized into Moses\" - they weren't Jews by the flesh</li><li class=\"d1\">church = <b>Israel</b>, everyone grafted into the root = <b>Abraham's faithfulness</b> (Romans)</li><li class=\"d1\">in Christ → <b>Abraham your father</b> (Galatians)</li><li class=\"d0\">So forefathers = in <b>faithfulness</b>, not genetic</li><li class=\"d1\">genetic forefathers didn't always serve God with pure conscience</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul mostly did serve with good conscience</li><li class=\"d0\">More evidence <b>not a new religion</b> - full <b>continuity</b> with Abraham, Moses, all the faithful</li></ul>"},{"t":369.04,"lv":3,"h":"Timothy: third-generation faith","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why he mentions it: applying to Timothy's life</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Timothy: \"let no one despise your <b>youth</b>\" - still a young man</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>third-generation</b> Christian</li><li class=\"d0\">Grandmother and mother faithful → \"same faithfulness is in you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Line of connection</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul an apostle by <b>will of God</b> - continuing story from <b>Abraham</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Timothy's own line: <b>mother and grandmother</b></li><li class=\"d1\">inheritance received \"quite literally within your family\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: where is Timothy from? - not told a city, will check</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Living tradition</b> - not just church tradition, back into OT</li><li class=\"d1\">macro scale and micro scale (his own family)</li></ul>"},{"t":468.98,"lv":2,"h":"1:6-7 - Laying on of hands, the Spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:6-7</b>: \"stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands\"; God gave not a spirit of <b>fear</b>, but <b>power, love, sound mind</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Laying on of hands</b> - also in 1 Timothy</li><li class=\"d1\">not throwing hands or a fight - <b>ordination</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Spirit</b> almost always capitalized in NT - not \"pep rally\" spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">= the <b>Holy Spirit</b> Timothy received from Paul's laying on of hands</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul ordained and set him apart</li><li class=\"d0\">Holy Spirit not fear but <b>power, love, sound mind</b> - these go together</li><li class=\"d1\">power and love not opposed; sound mind not opposed to love</li><li class=\"d1\">all three <b>cooperate</b></li><li class=\"d0\">You've received the Spirit → <b>make use of it</b>; don't receive \"grace of God in vain\"</li></ul>"},{"t":584.56,"lv":3,"h":"\"Stir up\" and the meaning of power","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Stir up</b> = don't let it <b>settle</b> like <b>sediment</b>; active, moving</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: how did they understand <b>power</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">why the three are together: <b>Caesar</b> had power - not so much love, often not sound mind (<b>Caligula, Nero</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Today these are opposed</li><li class=\"d1\">power/authority seen as evil or corrupting; <b>power = ability to make things happen</b></li><li class=\"d1\">love seen as passive - \"general goodwill\", acceptance, opposed to power</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God's love is active</b> - causes him to act</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>parental love</b>: parent acts out of love in ways the child may see as abusive power</li><li class=\"d1\">power used for love - bringing others to <b>maturity, perfection, completion</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sound mind</b>: we split reason/facts from feelings/love - Paul says not opposed</li><li class=\"d1\">love wants what's actually best → sound mind sees <b>what is good, who God is</b> → love motivates → <b>power and authority</b> make it happen</li></ul>"},{"t":800.04,"lv":3,"h":"Use what God has given","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Given all three ≠ will exercise all three - \"might sit at home and play Xbox\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul points to his <b>ordination</b>: go out and <b>use</b> what God gave</li><li class=\"d1\">don't let it settle like sediment, <b>rot</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"actualize it, be active, be alive, be involved\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>superpowers</b> or magical powers - not \"go do miracles\"</li></ul>"},{"t":859.71,"lv":2,"h":"1:8 - Don't be ashamed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:8</b>: don't be ashamed of the testimony, nor of me <b>his prisoner</b>; share in <b>sufferings for the gospel</b> according to the <b>power of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Juxtaposes with \"not a spirit of fear\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Don't be ashamed of preaching, or of Paul in prison <b>about to be executed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sufferings \"according to the <b>power of God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God's strength is made perfect in <b>weakness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">looks helpless, suffering, dying - but <b>God is doing something in and through this</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cross</b> = ultimate symbol</li><li class=\"d1\">naked man tortured to death by Roman state - weakness, humiliation</li><li class=\"d1\">in actuality the <b>glory of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Timothy must learn to see it in Paul's life <b>and his own</b></li><li class=\"d1\">when weak and powerless, God can act powerfully</li></ul>"},{"t":982.18,"lv":2,"h":"1:9 - God's purpose and grace","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:9</b>: God \"saved us and called us with a holy calling, not according to our works, but according to his own <b>purpose and grace</b>, given in Christ Jesus <b>before time began</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Here come all the Calvinists\" - but <b>context</b> is our friend</li><li class=\"d1\">continues the theme: God working in and through us</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"Timothy did a good job, God rewards him - get your sales figures up next quarter\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>1:1</b> - apostle by <b>will of God</b>: God has a <b>purpose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">calling Timothy to be the <b>means</b> through which he does it</li><li class=\"d0\">Not about Timothy's goals, <b>vision statement</b> for his church, bragging at career-end speeches</li><li class=\"d1\">about what <b>God</b> is doing and Timothy \"getting on board\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Grace</b> = the <b>divine energies</b>, God acting</li><li class=\"d1\">God has the purpose, God does it through you</li></ul>"},{"t":1102.84,"lv":3,"h":"Before time began","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Before the world began\" - \"it's really not about you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God's purpose <b>from the very beginning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gift given to Paul and Timothy: part of this <b>history, family, genealogy of faithful people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">part of what God has done since the beginning</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul asserts strongly that <b>Christ was around before time began</b></li><li class=\"d1\">aside: picking on a sceptic in the room</li></ul>"},{"t":1143.18,"lv":2,"h":"1:10-11 - Death abolished; gospel = victory","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:10</b>: now revealed by the <b>appearing of our Savior</b> Jesus Christ, who \"abolished death and brought <b>life and immortality</b> to light through the gospel\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Always God's purpose, plan - now <b>revealed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">before: \"a little unclear\" - images, glimpses; now fully revealed in <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Notice: <b>nothing about sin</b> or anyone being punished</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul focuses on Christ's <b>defeat and destruction of death</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Gospel</b> = ancient Greek word for <b>report of a victory</b></li><li class=\"d1\">appeal to victory idea: through Christ's victory, death abolished</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:11</b>: appointed <b>preacher, apostle, teacher of the Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his part in the plan: proclaim who Christ is, what he did, to <b>all nations</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1259.86,"lv":2,"h":"1:12 - I know whom I have believed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:12</b>: \"for this reason I also suffer... not ashamed, for <b>I know whom I have believed</b>... able to keep what I have committed to him until <b>that day</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not ashamed because he knows <b>what God is doing</b> - and more, <b>knows Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the one he has faithfully followed → <b>trusts</b> him</li><li class=\"d0\">\"What he's committed\" - like <b>treasures in heaven</b> (1 Timothy, to the wealthy members)</li><li class=\"d1\">committed to Christ for <b>that day</b> = day of the Lord, <b>judgment</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not working for a reward here\" - knows Christ will be <b>faithful to me</b> when that day comes</li></ul>"},{"t":1347.39,"lv":3,"h":"Discussion: preaching life while suffering","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: he preaches life, immortality - yet suffers?</li><li class=\"d1\">because that's how <b>Christ defeated it</b> - by suffering and dying</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Four Spiritual Laws</b> tracts (Campus Crusade) - \"I'm an old man now\"</li><li class=\"d1\">first law: <b>\"God loves you and has a wonderful plan for your life\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">joke: picture of <b>Ignatius</b> eaten by lions, or <b>John the Forerunner</b> beheaded, with that caption</li><li class=\"d0\">People don't want to hear \"<b>pick up your cross</b> and follow Christ\" - to be crucified</li><li class=\"d1\">that's what it means: suffer, live the life Christ lived - <b>rejection, mockery, beatings, execution</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1472.43,"lv":3,"h":"Martyrdom as testimony","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Willing to die for the gospel → must <b>believe</b> what Paul says here</li><li class=\"d1\">that at Christ's throne <b>he will be faithful to you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">willingness to go to death = <b>ultimate testimony</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paradox - central to Christian idea; irrational → appealing: it <b>transcends reason</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1523.47,"lv":2,"h":"Nietzsche and the inversion","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Nietzsche</b> = \"the greatest critic of Christianity\" - only critic who <b>really understood</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">he was a <b>classical philologist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">saw Greeks and Romans: power, strength, beauty, wealth, \"right\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christianity <b>inverts</b> it: those people evil; <b>peasants, suffering, sick</b> good; \"executed person is God\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He hated it - but at least hated Christianity <b>for what it actually is</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: commit to the transcendent good → willfully <b>sacrifice</b> worldly comforts like Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you give up good things to get <b>better</b> things\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1634.11,"lv":2,"h":"Asceticism and the West","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>West doesn't get asceticism</b> - \"non-controversial statement\"</li><li class=\"d1\">assumes what you give up is <b>bad</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Augustinian tradition</b>: celibacy good because sex always \"kind of bad\"; money, possessions likewise</li><li class=\"d0\">No category for actual asceticism - supposed to stop sinning, giving up sin isn't asceticism</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Asceticism</b> = giving up a <b>good thing</b> (material, lesser good) to pursue what is <b>truly good, eternal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same problem in <b>ethics</b>: very <b>law-based</b> ethic in the West</li><li class=\"d1\">everything either bad or good; only one good - from <b>Platonism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"distinction implies opposition\": if this is good, anything else at least a little bad</li><li class=\"d1\">good has to be one, \"because Plato told us so\" - can't be two equally good things</li></ul>"},{"t":1751.42,"lv":3,"h":"Protestant negative asceticism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Protestantism: <b>negative asceticism</b> - what you <b>don't</b> do (drink, dance, cards)</li><li class=\"d1\">not stuff you actively go out and do</li><li class=\"d0\">No place for <b>celibacy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sex bad outside marriage, good inside → wrong not to do it in marriage; only two categories</li><li class=\"d1\">unmarried adult gets looked at \"like something's wrong with you... weirdo\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if marriage is good, everything else \"not quite as good\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Asceticism swept aside - practicing it says what you abstain from is <b>bad</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1840.31,"lv":2,"h":"The ultimate good","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Real moral problem isn't people doing evil on purpose</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle</b>, opening of <b>Nicomachean Ethics</b>: \"everyone always does what is good\" - disagree what good is</li><li class=\"d0\">We treat <b>temporal goods</b> as the ultimate good to the exclusion of all else</li><li class=\"d1\">only <b>God</b> is the ultimate good</li><li class=\"d1\">Scripture clear (unpopular): pursue God to exclusion of all else - love father/mother, spouse, children <b>more than me</b> → \"not worthy of me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Others pursue <b>money, success, fame, pleasure</b> to the exclusion of all else - more than children, spouse, parents</li><li class=\"d1\">wrong ultimate good → <b>doing evil to everyone else</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pursue God → spirit of <b>sound mind, love, power</b> → we don't do evil to them</li><li class=\"d1\">works in the exact opposite way - good, kindness, love, <b>even when we choose God over what they want</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2011.64,"lv":2,"h":"Platonism in Western theology","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Western theology: <b>Platonist period</b> after <b>Augustine</b> - <b>Boethius</b>, culminating in <b>Anselm</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I don't think it ever recovered\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Aristotelian period after - \"theoretically better\"; but Platonist core still the <b>bedrock</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in understanding of the <b>Trinity</b> etc.</li><li class=\"d1\">Aristotle nuances and explicates but doesn't question or topple</li><li class=\"d0\">Core presuppositions took Western theology \"in the wrong direction\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>unity, simplicity</b> = perfection per Plato → perfect good must have it → no <b>multiplicity or variation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2121.08,"lv":3,"h":"Maximus vs Pyrrhus: freedom in the eschaton","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>St Maximus the Confessor</b> dispute with <b>Pyrrhus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Maximus: we'll have <b>free will</b> in the life of the world to come</li><li class=\"d1\">Pyrrhus: no - we'd only do good, good has to be <b>one</b> → no choices, no variation, all the same, \"perfect and simple\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"That sounds awful\" - angels? why more than one?</li><li class=\"d1\">fallen ones aside, freely doing good - <b>multiple goods</b>; variation that's still good</li></ul>"},{"t":2184.65,"lv":3,"h":"The saints: variation in goodness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Clearest example: the <b>saints</b></li><li class=\"d1\">icons look similar in style - all conformed to the <b>likeness of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but all <b>individual persons</b>, not clones: different lives, gifts, experiences</li><li class=\"d1\">\"thousands of ways to be just like Christ\"</li><li class=\"d0\">→ freedom and variation - \"toss Plato in the dustbin\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Different ≠ bad</li></ul>"},{"t":2277.23,"lv":2,"h":"What is free will?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Evil = <b>failure to do good</b>: everybody trying to do good, confused about what it is</li><li class=\"d1\">in heaven we won't fail at what we're trying</li><li class=\"d0\">Modern Western will = <b>making choices</b>: Coke or Pepsi, Diet Coke or Coke Zero</li><li class=\"d1\">American thing - \"two of everything\": Xbox/PlayStation, Ford/Chevy</li><li class=\"d0\">Maximus: that's a <b>second movement of the will</b> after the fall - \"a <b>bug, not a feature</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">deliberating to figure out the right thing = a lesser state</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ</b> didn't have it; <b>God</b> doesn't make choices - \"sorry, Calvinist\" - he knows, acts eternally according to his <b>nature</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why we won't sin in the world to come: <b>perfect free will</b></li><li class=\"d1\">free to be and become who God created us to be; free from sin</li></ul>"},{"t":2416.42,"lv":3,"h":"Will as function of nature: the acorn","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ancient view: will is a function of <b>nature</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle</b>'s <i>thelema</i> = will - even trees, rocks have it</li><li class=\"d1\">anything with a nature has a will: internal force moving it toward that nature</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acorn</b> and oak: same nature, look different</li><li class=\"d1\">will = internal living force driving it to grow toward the sun, to <b>full maturity</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sin</b> = rot, moss, parasites that stop it becoming a healthy oak</li><li class=\"d1\">remove sin → will is <b>free to become</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same with us: God created us to become someone; sin comes in <b>parasitically</b></li><li class=\"d1\">purified and healed → free to grow into that person</li></ul>"},{"t":2543.84,"lv":2,"h":"Q: Tree of knowledge and Adam and Eve","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: how does this tie to the <b>tree of the knowledge of good and evil</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Second Temple literature (e.g. <b>1 Enoch</b>): tree still in <b>paradise</b>, not evil</li><li class=\"d1\">knowledge Adam and Eve <b>weren't ready for</b></li><li class=\"d0\">East vs West: East sees Adam and Eve as <b>innocent, like newborn children</b> at start of the journey</li><li class=\"d1\">West: <b>perfect</b> → \"how could perfect beings sin?\" - all kinds of problems</li><li class=\"d0\">God to angels: man \"now like us, knowing good and evil\"</li><li class=\"d1\">knowing it didn't make God or elect angels evil</li><li class=\"d1\">time would have come when humanity was <b>mature enough</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Devil: eat it, \"you'll become like God\" - \"do the mature grown-up thing now\"</li><li class=\"d1\">temptation to children; on your own effort - <b>circumvent growing and becoming</b>, \"I want to be like God now\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2675.55,"lv":2,"h":"1:13-14 - Pattern of sound words","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:13-14</b>: \"hold fast the <b>pattern of sound words</b>\" heard from me in faith and love; keep the good thing committed to you by the <b>Holy Spirit</b> who dwells in us</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pattern of sound words</b> = way of describing the <b>gospel</b>, who Christ is, who you are</li><li class=\"d1\">not exact words to recite - a <b>pattern</b>: way of seeing, talking, describing</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you heard it from me as you traveled with me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel was committed to Paul, he spent life proclaiming it</li><li class=\"d1\">now committed <b>through him to Timothy</b> - hold on</li></ul>"},{"t":2752.72,"lv":2,"h":"1:15-18 - Those who turned away; Onesiphorus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:15</b>: all in <b>Asia</b> turned away, incl. <b>Phygelus and Hermogenes</b> - naming names</li><li class=\"d1\">Asia = Asia Minor, now <b>Turkey</b> - churches founded in Acts</li><li class=\"d1\">\"thought I had a lot of friends there\" - supporters who flaked out</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:16-18</b>: <b>Onesiphorus</b> and household did the opposite</li><li class=\"d1\">often refreshed Paul, not ashamed of his <b>chain</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in Rome sought him out \"very zealously\" and found him</li><li class=\"d1\">ministered to him at <b>Ephesus</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul asks mercy for him \"in that day\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2836.15,"lv":3,"h":"Echoes of Jesus' teaching","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Another connection to Christ's teaching</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sheep and the goats</b>: \"you visited me when I was in prison\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He always points these out - folks (incl. <b>all Muslims</b>) claim Paul teaches something totally different from Jesus</li><li class=\"d1\">actually \"all these overlaps all the time\"</li></ul>"}]});
